---
scope: src/cli/sync
status: aligned
description: The user-typed terminal verb that propagates the host's brain config into the host's MCP launcher entry, refusing to write while any slot is unwired.
intent: >
  sync is the terminal verb the user types to propagate the host's brain
  config into the host's MCP launcher entry. It is the only mutation
  surface for that entry across the package, and runs only when the user
  invokes it deliberately. Sync's contract is faithful propagation of
  an authoritative source — but a config carrying any unwired slot is
  broken state, not propagatable state. When any slot is unwired, sync
  refuses to write, names which slot, and routes the user to wire it;
  it never coerces, drops, or substitutes the unwired signal into the
  launch command.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Propagates the user's authoritative brain config into the host's launcher entry faithfully — what the user wrote is what runs, with no rewording, summarization, or reshaping along the way.
    - Refuses to write whenever the brain config carries an unwired slot, names which slot is unwired in plain terminal output, and points the user at the wiring command. The launcher entry is left untouched on this path.
    - Idempotent — running sync twice in succession on a wired config produces no second write and reports no changes. Running it after a hand-edit of the brain config writes once and reports the edit.
    - Runs only when the user types its name in their terminal. No code path elsewhere in the package mutates the launcher's brain entry; every change the user observes is one they typed.
    - Preserves every other launcher entry in the host's MCP config verbatim. The brain entry is the only key sync touches; sibling entries the user wired by hand survive a sync run unchanged.
  undesired:
    - Coercing, dropping, or substituting an unwired slot at the boundary so the launcher entry ends up with a wired-shaped value the user never authored. The unwired signal must reach the user, not get laundered into something the launcher will silently fail on.
    - A second mutation surface for the host's brain launcher entry anywhere else in the package — auto-running on server boot, on install, on diagnostic calls, or as a "helpful" tail of any other command. The user's typed invocation is the only acceptable trigger.
    - Reverse-direction sync that reads the host's launcher config and writes back to the brain config. Drift always resolves outward from the user's authoritative source, never inward from the file the user may hand-edit for debugging.
    - Treating sync as interactive — prompting the user for a value to fill an unwired slot, or asking where their brain lives. Sync runs in plain terminal context with no agent surface; the brain config on disk is the complete input set.
    - Mutating anything under the user-owned brain config directory — patching it, tidying its formatting, filling unwired slots in place, or scaffolding it from scratch when missing. Sync reads that directory and never writes to it.
    - Overwriting unrelated entries in the host's launcher config. Sync touches the brain entry and only the brain entry; every other key the user has wired must survive verbatim.
---
